      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. INTRODUCTION TO ADVERBS"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Adverbs modify verbs, adjectives, or other adverbs to indicate time, place, manner, degree, or frequency.",
        "Adverbs of frequency (always, never, seldom) are placed before the main verb but after the helping verb 'be'.",
        "'Enough' is uniquely placed AFTER the adjective or adverb it modifies, but BEFORE a noun.",
        "Negative adverbs like 'hardly', 'scarcely', and 'barely' must never be paired with 'not' (Double Negative Rule).",
        "Confusing adverb pairs like 'hard/hardly' and 'late/lately' carry completely distinct semantic meanings."
      ],
      "id": "anv7lv2"
    },
